# EVERY text file checks out LF, on every platform, whatever core.autocrlf says. The
# plugin is installed by cloning this repository, so the bytes a checkout writes ARE
# the shipped product: a command's or an agent's markdown is prose a model reads and
# pays for by the byte, and a CRLF checkout (windows-latest, Git for Windows'
# default autocrlf) grew every line of it by one, pushing the reviewer's start and
# the phase run form over the ceilings `tools/measure-context.py --gate` holds. The
# pipeline also commits JSON and JSONL byte for byte (the manifest, the journal, the
# evidence), and selftests pin other files byte for byte, so a per-extension list
# was the wrong unit: each type below joined it only after a Windows run had failed
# on it. `text=auto` leaves git's own binary detection in
# charge, so images stay untouched. `measure-context.py`'s selftest asks git which
# tracked text file does not resolve to eol=lf and fails naming it.
* text=auto eol=lf
# The rules below came first and are narrower; each keeps the reason it was added,
# and each sets `text` outright rather than leaving the file to git's detection.
#
# plugins/audit/scripts/ui/* are byte-precise build inputs: _panel_ui.py and
# _report_ui.py load them with io.open(..., encoding="utf-8", newline="") —
# deliberately no newline translation, because the assembled UI_HTML/_CSS/
# _SCRIPT byte-identity proofs in each module's --selftest depend on the
# exact bytes on disk. A CRLF checkout (e.g. windows-latest CI with
# core.autocrlf rewriting the repo) silently shifts every selftest pin that
# spans a line boundary. Pin these assets to LF so every checkout, on every
# platform, hands the readers the same bytes.
plugins/audit/scripts/ui/** text eol=lf
# ...and the token layer WITH them, though it sits one directory up. It is not a
# `ui/` asset by path, but it is one by use: `ui_surface_digests()` hashes it as a
# member of BOTH surfaces, reading raw bytes. Unpinned, a CRLF checkout gave it
# different bytes and therefore a different digest for every surface at once, so
# `screenshot_capture_drift()` reddened every committed image on windows-latest and
# no re-capture from any other platform could clear it. The rule above already
# stated the reason; the file it applies to was one directory short.
plugins/audit/scripts/_ui_theme.py text eol=lf
# Shell scripts and workflow files are read LINE BY LINE by checkers that treat a
# trailing carriage return as part of the line, the way the shell itself does - so
# a CRLF checkout turned `tools/verify.sh`'s and `ci.yml`'s exact call lines into
# lines no checker recognised, and `check-rendered-artifacts.py` refused them on
# windows-latest. A script that sh runs wants LF anyway: a CR ends up inside the
# last word of every line.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
